# The food catalog

The catalog is your own list of the things you actually eat, with the numbers
you have worked out for them. It lives under **Food** in the sidebar, and it is
shared across everyone with access to your data — one household, one list. Your
_favorites_ within it are personal.

## What a food is

A food is a name plus a set of numbers for one portion of it:

| Field | Notes |
|-------|-------|
| **Name** | "Greek yogurt, plain". Required. |
| **Carbs** | Grams per portion. The number that does the work. |
| **Per** and **unit** | The portion this food's numbers describe: 100 g, 1 pcs, 250 ml, 1 serving. |
| **GI** | Low, medium or high. Defaults to medium. |
| **Fat**, **protein**, **energy** | Optional. Energy is entered as kcal in the editor. |
| **Category** and **subcategory** | Free-form grouping — "Breakfast", "Dairy", whatever suits you. |

<Screenshot id="food-composer" />

New foods start at 100 g, medium GI. Everything scales from the portion: define
a food as 12 g of carb per 100 g and Nocturne works out 1.5 portions for you
when you use it.

<Callout type="tip" title="Define the portion you actually measure">
  If you weigh your pasta, define the food per 100 g. If you count biscuits,
  define it per 1 pcs. The portion is not a nutrition-label convention — it is
  whatever unit stops you doing arithmetic at the table.
</Callout>

## Working the list

The **Food** page is one long searchable table. Search matches names, categories
and subcategories. You can filter to favorites only, sort by name, carbs or
recency, and edit rows in place — a food's carbs are one click and a number away
from being corrected.

<Screenshot
  id="food-catalog"
  callouts={[
    { anchor: "search", label: "Search names and categories" },
    { anchor: "favorites", label: "Your starred foods only" },
    { anchor: "sort", label: "Name, carbs or recency" },
  ]}
/>

**Favorites** are per person. Starring a food moves it up your list without
touching anyone else's. **Recents** are shared: the foods most recently used in
attributions across the account, so the picker offers what the household has
been eating rather than what you personally starred six months ago.

## Quick picks

A quick pick is a saved bundle of foods — the same breakfast, the four things
that make up a school lunch — stored as a single catalog entry containing a list
of foods and how many portions of each. Picking it applies the lot at once.

Quick picks carry two flags worth knowing: **hidden**, which keeps one out of
the picker without deleting it, and **hide after use**, which retires it
automatically once you have used it. They also carry a display position, so you
can order the ones you reach for most.

Quick picks are a legacy Nightscout concept and Nocturne stores them
compatibly, which is why they are the same kind of record as an ordinary food
rather than a separate thing.

## Deleting a food that is in use

Foods get referenced by attributions on your meal history, sometimes years of
them. Deleting one therefore asks a question first: Nocturne counts how many
attributions point at the food and tells you the number. You then choose what
happens to them:

- **Clear the references** (the default). The attribution lines stay on your
  meals, with their carbs and portions intact, but no longer point at a catalog
  entry. Your history keeps its shape; the deleted food simply stops being
  linkable.
- **Remove the attributions.** The lines go too. Your carb entries keep their
  totals — deleting an attribution never changes how many carbs were recorded —
  but the breakdown of what those carbs were is gone, and the carbs return to
  being unspecified.

<Callout type="warning" title="Deleting is not correcting">
  If a food's numbers are simply wrong, edit it. Editing updates the catalog
  entry in place, and existing attributions keep the carb figures that were
  recorded at the time. Deleting and re-adding gives you a new food, an
  identical-looking list, and a history that points at the wrong one of the two.
</Callout>

## Through the API

The catalog is available three ways, all backed by the same records:

| Surface | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `/api/v1/food` | Nightscout-compatible, including `/regular` and `/quickpicks`. |
| `/api/v3/food` | Nightscout v3, with the modified-since history endpoint. |
| `/api/v4/foods` | Native, with favorites, recents and attribution counts. |

Writes need `food.readwrite`; reads need `food.read`, which a `health.read`
grant expands to include. A single upload that contains the same item twice —
same name, portion, unit and numbers — stores it once.
